WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:04.296
- [Male Voice] This is Ed Glaser.

00:00:04.296 --> 00:00:07.078
He's a film historian
with a peculiar specialty.

00:00:07.078 --> 00:00:10.429
- I talk about, highlight
and occasionally

00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:13.707
re-release foreign rip-offs and remakes.

00:00:13.707 --> 00:00:16.259
- [Male Voice] Remakes like
Turkish Rambo, Nigerian Titanic,

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:18.486
and even Egyptian Rocky
Horror Picture Show.

00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:20.818
But there's one that
stands out from the rest.

00:00:20.818 --> 00:00:22.907
- The Holy Grail of these kinds of films is

00:00:22.907 --> 00:00:25.747
Çetin İnanç’s ‘The
Man who Saves the World,’

00:00:25.747 --> 00:00:28.914
better known as the Turkish Star Wars.

00:00:34.608 --> 00:00:37.029
- [Male Voice] Turkish Star
Wars is a 1980s action film

00:00:37.029 --> 00:00:39.789
that tells the story of two
Turkish space adventurers

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.219
who crash land on a desert planet

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.698
and decide to overthrow
its evil, tyrannical ruler.

00:00:44.698 --> 00:00:47.840
- I’d never really seen anything like it.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.000
It's a little bit like a fever dream.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.240
- [Male Voice] And it's
famous for, well, this.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.880
- [Ed] It's called Turkish Star Wars,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.848
not because of the plot,

00:00:57.848 --> 00:01:02.080
but because it actually
lifts footage from Star Wars

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.247
to sell the illusion that
they're flying fighters in space.

00:01:09.949 --> 00:01:10.887
- [Male Voice] When the
film was in theaters,

00:01:10.887 --> 00:01:12.379
only a handful of prints were made

00:01:12.379 --> 00:01:14.308
and, given that there was
virtually no film preservation

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:16.320
in Turkey at the time,
every copy was thought

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.688
to have been destroyed.

00:01:17.688 --> 00:01:19.187
But, after decades in obscurity,

00:01:19.187 --> 00:01:21.400
media reports surfaced
of a rogue projectionist

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.408
who kept the only
existing copy of the film

00:01:23.408 --> 00:01:25.006
in his personal collection.

00:01:25.006 --> 00:01:28.440
Ed made it his mission to
restore the film to its glory.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.526
- Once I got hold of the film,

00:01:30.526 --> 00:01:33.219
I got in touch with a company,
who was very, very excited

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:35.148
about scanning and preserving it.

00:01:35.148 --> 00:01:36.446
Obviously, it's 30 years old,

00:01:36.446 --> 00:01:38.187
it's in a little bit rough shape.

00:01:38.187 --> 00:01:39.645
What I want to be able to do

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:43.275
is restore it to the
best possible condition

00:01:43.275 --> 00:01:46.246
and, ideally, in front
of as many people's eyes

00:01:46.246 --> 00:01:47.746
as I possibly can.

00:01:49.977 --> 00:01:50.955
- [Male Voice] Yeah, but why
go through all this trouble

00:01:50.955 --> 00:01:52.747
for Turkish Star Wars?

00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:54.164
- It's so unique.

00:01:55.057 --> 00:01:59.224
Visually, it's so lurid and
vivid and wild and crazy

00:02:00.147 --> 00:02:03.446
and there's just nothing else like it.

00:02:03.446 --> 00:02:06.646
The first time I saw it
was on a screen this big

00:02:06.646 --> 00:02:08.257
and now I wanna see it on the big screen,

00:02:08.257 --> 00:02:10.964
the way that it was in 1982.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.515
- [Male Voice] How do you say
"may the force be with you"

00:02:12.515 --> 00:02:13.432
in Turkish?

